```nginx
upstream app_servers {
    server 10.0.1.10:8080 max_fails=3 fail_timeout=30s;
    server 10.0.1.11:8080 max_fails=3 fail_timeout=30s;
    server 10.0.1.12:8080 max_fails=3 fail_timeout=30s;
}

server {
    location /health {
        proxy_pass http://app_servers;
    }
}
```

A load balancer periodically pokes each backend — an HTTP GET to `/health`, a TCP connect, whatever the config specifies — and pulls any server that fails enough consecutive checks out of rotation. This is what turns 'a server crashed' from an outage into a non-event: traffic just quietly stops going there.

The two knobs that matter are how often you check and how many failures you tolerate before pulling a server. Check too rarely and a dead server keeps eating real traffic for longer than necessary; check too aggressively and normal jitter starts flapping healthy servers in and out of rotation.

```json
// During migration: both fields present
{
  "user_id": 42,
  "email": "jane@example.com",   // old field, still populated
  "contact_email": "jane@example.com"  // new field, same value
}
```

Adding a new field to an API response is safe for existing consumers — anything not looking for the new field simply ignores it. Removing or renaming a field is not safe, because any consumer still reading the old name breaks the instant it disappears, and in a system with independently deployed services, you generally cannot guarantee every consumer has already migrated.

The **expand-contract** pattern resolves this without a coordinated deploy: first *expand* by adding the new field alongside the old one (both populated, as above), let every consumer migrate to the new field on their own schedule, and only once nothing is reading the old field anymore do you *contract* by removing it. It's slower than a direct rename, but it's what actually preserves independent deployability — the entire point of having service boundaries in the first place.

```sql
BEGIN;
  INSERT INTO orders (id, user_id, amount, status)
  VALUES ('ord_1123', 42, 4999, 'created');

  INSERT INTO outbox (id, aggregate_id, event_type, payload)
  VALUES ('evt_9910', 'ord_1123', 'OrderCreated', '{"amount": 4999}');
COMMIT;
```

The **transactional outbox** sidesteps the dual-write problem by not actually performing two operations against two systems at all — both the business row (`orders`) and the event describing it (`outbox`) are written to the *same* database, in the *same* local transaction. Since that's a single database, the database's own atomicity guarantee applies: either both rows exist, or neither does. There's no window where one succeeded and the other didn't.

A separate process (a poller, or change data capture reading the outbox table's own changes) then reads unpublished rows from the outbox and relays them to the message broker, retrying on failure and marking them published once the broker confirms. That relay step can safely retry, because relaying an already-published event twice is a duplicate-delivery problem the consumer's idempotency handles — not a dual-write problem, which is exactly the improvement: it converts an unreliable, unretryable dual write into a reliable local write plus an ordinary, retryable at-least-once relay.

```json
{
  "hooks": {
    "PostToolUse": [
      { "matcher": "Edit", "command": "ruff check --fix ." }
    ]
  }
}
```

This runs the linter after every edit, unconditionally — not because the model decided to, but because the harness executes it as part of handling that event. Any rule of the shape 'every time X happens, always do Y' belongs here rather than in a prompt, because a hook has a 100% compliance rate by construction and a prompt does not.

```json
{
  "permissions": {
    "allow": ["Bash(git status)", "Bash(git diff)", "Bash(pytest -q)"]
  }
}
```

An allowlist entry in settings is a one-time decision that then holds for every session afterward, rather than a decision remade by clicking 'approve' every time the same safe command comes up. This is the harness-level version of the earlier permission subtopic's narrow-allowlist advice — written down once, instead of re-litigated per session.
